    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for this. i'm particularly worried about \"bad faith\" situations where a supplier might try to hide information about shortages or sneak in price hikes through fine print. how can we draft the language to specifically target those kinds of hidden threats or bad actors in the supply chain?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! Trouble spots in a supply agreement can be addressed with the following steps:\n\n1. Specify qualification and knowledge: Ensure that suppliers only supply you in good faith for which granting you the material in question was agreed upon, and insist that they present themselves and their business reliably when discussing potential contracts.\n2. Make obligations for specific performance: By keeping not just on promises, making the supplier default on not fulfilling the desired performance, it stimulates more trust between you and the supplier.\n3. Include progress updates: Require the supplier to give updates about the availability of goods or reserves; place the shared focus on both parties to diagnose whether there are issues arising.\n4. Allow for addendum: Draft that parties may negotiate and sign changes to the terms of the agreement to add new supply-related information or other details as needed. This ensures that all details are transparent and agreed upon by both parties at any given time.\n5. Allow for clear communication: Stipulate that suppliers must communicate trial orders, possible setbacks, and also substantial price changes; after all, a significant market change will affect the supply agreement, too.\n6. Place emphasis on honesty: By unequivocally stating that it is expected the supplier will follow applicable laws and provides honest disclosures/restr",
